1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:03,852
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Sixteen.

2
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:11,415
In any triangle, if one side is extended, the exterior angle is greater than either opposite interior angle.

3
00:00:11,415 --> 00:00:22,130
Given triangle A B C with side B C extended to point D, we prove that exterior angle A C D is greater than either opposite interior angle B A C or C B A.

4
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:28,340
Using Proposition Ten, bisect segment A C at point E so that A E equals E C.

5
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:34,968
Join segment B E and extend it to F, cutting off E F equal to B E by Proposition Three.

6
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:39,920
Then join segment F C, and extend segment A C through to point G.

7
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:44,927
By Proposition Fifteen, vertical angles A E B and F E C are equal.

8
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:55,561
Since sides A E and E B equal sides C E and E F, Proposition Four proves that triangles A B E and C F E are congruent, so angle B A E equals angle E C F.

9
00:00:55,561 --> 00:01:03,902
Because the whole exterior angle E C D is greater than its part E C F, exterior angle A C D is greater than angle B A C.

10
00:01:03,902 --> 00:01:13,980
Similarly, bisecting B C proves that vertical angle B C G, and therefore angle A C D, is also greater than angle C B A, as was required to prove.
